import{_ as r,C as t,o as s,c as o,E as a,a2 as c}from"./chunks/framework.Bx6eOp9J.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Why the same not-exercised state can receive different scores","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Why the same not-exercised state can receive different scores","date":"2026-10-26","column":"open-source-engineering","category":"daily","article_type":"engineering-insight","edition":"research-center","research_question":"当未触发状态按任务结果重新记分时，应保留哪些原始状态和归因边界？","research_question_en":"When not-exercised states are resolved using task outcome, what raw states and attribution boundaries should evaluation preserve?","summary":"A pinned source function resolves the same not-exercised state to one or zero using task outcome. Interpretation must retain raw evidence, resolution rules and outcome rather than treat zeros as confirmed defects.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32285","https://github.com/TraceLite-AI/TraceTrial-Bench/blob/65dd7150b06f6c5cf345c70de8ca0348db62ef93/harnessbench/llm_judge.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Lorelys/maat-benchmarks/main/CORRECTIONS.md","https://arxiv.org/html/2610.30043"],"pageClass":"runtime-v6-article","item_id":"SIG-20261026-003","lifecycle":"Published","cover":"/assets/covers/daily-2026-10-26-sig-20261026-003-2026-10-26-score-evidence.png","evidence_status":"Completed","citation_status":"Completed","editing_status":"Completed","publication_authorized":true},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/engineering/2026-10-26-sig-20261026-003.md","filePath":"en/engineering/2026-10-26-sig-20261026-003.md","lastUpdated":1793018684000}'),d={name:"en/engineering/2026-10-26-sig-20261026-003.md"};function l(h,e,u,p,m,f){const n=t("ArticleCover"),i=t("ArticleTableScroll");return s(),o("div",null,[a(n,{image:"/assets/covers/daily-2026-10-26-sig-20261026-003-2026-10-26-score-evidence.png",kicker:"Research",title:"Why the same not-exercised state can receive different scores",summary:"A pinned source function resolves the same not-exercised state to one or zero using task outcome. Interpretation must retain raw evidence, resolution rules and outcome rather than treat zeros as confirmed defects.",version:"2026-10-26",languageHref:"/zh/engineering/2026-10-26-sig-20261026-003",languageLabel:"中文"}),a(i,{language:"en"}),e[0]||(e[0]=c('<h1 id="why-the-same-not-exercised-state-can-receive-different-scores" tabindex="-1">Why the same not-exercised state can receive different scores <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-the-same-not-exercised-state-can-receive-different-scores" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why the same not-exercised state can receive different scores&quot;">​</a></h1><p>How should an execution-harness capability be scored when it was not exercised? In one pinned TraceTrial revision, the answer changes with task outcome: one when all Agent deliverables pass, zero otherwise. The raw harness state stays the same; the resolution condition changes.[1][2]</p><p>Our internal source experiment executed that function. It exposes an interpretation boundary: storing Agent and Harness layers separately does not make their scores definitionally independent. A zero needs its raw state, applicable rule and task outcome; confirming a defect or propagation mechanism requires further evidence.</p><h2 id="the-dependency-in-a-sixteen-cell-input-matrix" tabindex="-1">The dependency in a sixteen-cell input matrix <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-dependency-in-a-sixteen-cell-input-matrix" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The dependency in a sixteen-cell input matrix&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On October 26, 2026, our internal experiment extracted the unchanged resolver from a pinned revision and supplied synthetic raw states under four Agent-score conditions. This table contains all sixteen combinations.[2]</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Raw Harness state</th><th>No Agent score object</th><th>Object with empty items</th><th>All items pass</th><th>An item fails</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2: absent or not exercised</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>3: device malfunction</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This is an internal function probe, not semantic re-adjudication of real traces. Raw zero and one remain invariant; state two depends on task outcome; state three becomes zero when an Agent-score object exists. With no object, the function returns raw results unchanged. Missing objects and empty item lists are different conditions.</p><p>The resolver retains original-score and reason fields on changed records, preserving traceability. A downstream store containing only final zeros and ones would lose distinctions among not exercised, malfunction and an ordinary zero. What we observe is a dependency in the scoring definition, not an estimate of statistical association across the actual corpus.</p><h2 id="a-utility-rule-is-not-a-defect-diagnosis" tabindex="-1">A utility rule is not a defect diagnosis <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-utility-rule-is-not-a-defect-diagnosis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A utility rule is not a defect diagnosis&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The source gives an intentional design rationale: if a capability was absent and the task failed, charge the harness; if the model compensated and completed the task, do not penalize the harness for that absence. This can be a deliberate utility-scoring choice. Our experiment does not establish that the choice is wrong. Its outputs must retain the meaning of that choice.</p><p>A zero can mean that evidence of satisfaction is missing, rather than identify a located implementation defect. The paper reports 1,065 Agent-pass executions with at least one Harness zero, about 31.1% of 3,420 selected executions. That is not 1,065 independently confirmed harness defects. We did not re-adjudicate those cases.[1, §§3–4 and Table 10]</p><p>The paper&#39;s automatic propagation labels match failure endpoints to declared conditions, producing candidate associations. A propagation judgment additionally needs endpoint verification, visible intermediate distortion, event order and competing explanations. A failed outcome and a harness shortfall do not establish causal direction through their intersection alone. Our interpretation is that utility scores may serve their declared objective while causal attribution requires a separate review chain.[1, §3 and propagation appendix]</p><h2 id="halts-and-progress-cannot-substitute-for-final-outcomes" tabindex="-1">Halts and progress cannot substitute for final outcomes <a class="header-anchor" href="#halts-and-progress-cannot-substitute-for-final-outcomes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Halts and progress cannot substitute for final outcomes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Two other studies already examined in the Research Object supply different boundaries. Their effects cannot be pooled with this matrix.</p><p>The Maat authors&#39; correction audit classifies 94 intervention halts as 45 injected-defect catches, 13 real non-injected-defect catches, one legitimate escalation and 35 false alarms. The roughly 37% false-alarm fraction uses those halts as its denominator, not all 522 original trials. A halt alone cannot establish correct protection; defect presence and attributable triggering evidence still matter.[3]</p><p>The authors also report replaying 573 saved outputs offline after fixes. Those are author-reported saved-output replays, not new live model trials in this run or additional live repetitions of the original experiment. The complete conductor is unavailable; we claim no full-system replication.</p><p>TARSphere distinguishes accumulated stage progress from final success. In the authors&#39; account, completed actions and delivered messages advance task state. A terminal fall or object loss can veto success while previously earned progress remains. Ordinary contact does not automatically mean failure. Progress, final success and safety outcomes therefore require distinct interpretation.[4]</p><p>These studies examine different things: a resolver handles not-exercised states, a halt audit examines whether protection was justified, and an embodied evaluation distinguishes progress from completion. They share an evidence-semantics problem, not tasks, denominators or apparatus. They do not support a combined system ranking.</p><h2 id="keep-the-raw-record-beside-its-derived-score" tabindex="-1">Keep the raw record beside its derived score <a class="header-anchor" href="#keep-the-raw-record-beside-its-derived-score" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Keep the raw record beside its derived score&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Our bounded engineering recommendation is to retain raw state, resolved score, resolution reason, task outcome and scoring version together. Reasons for non-exercise and unavailable evidence should remain inspectable. A changed utility rule can then potentially recompute scores without rewriting observations. This is a design recommendation, not a measured large-scale benefit.</p><p>Correct delivery, satisfied process obligations and attributable protective halts can each remain inspectable axes. Propagation review additionally needs event order, intermediate state and alternative explanations. Our experiment executes one existing function on constructed inputs; it measures neither semantic-Judge agreement, selector recall nor candidate-attribution precision, and it runs no embodied simulator. The unresolved empirical question is how much these raw distinctions change final judgments across real cases.</p><h2 id="evidence-and-sources" tabindex="-1">Evidence and sources <a class="header-anchor" href="#evidence-and-sources" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Evidence and sources&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Public primary-source facts here concern inspectable rules and code behavior; a project&#39;s own material is not automatically independent evidence. Internal experimental evidence means our synthetic-input function probe. Source-reported findings are the authors&#39; experiments and corrections. The different teams did not reproduce the same claim for one another. Interpretations are bounded recommendations. Publication, citation and code availability do not establish validation; executable rules do not establish correct attribution.</p><ol><li>TraceTrial research team, research paper, October 2026 version: <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32285" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">full paper</a>, §§3–4, Tables 4/10 and scoring/propagation appendices. Intersections and candidate associations retain the authors&#39; definitions.</li><li>TraceTrial maintainers, public source code, revision <code>65dd7150b06f6c5cf345c70de8ca0348db62ef93</code>: <a href="https://github.com/TraceLite-AI/TraceTrial-Bench/blob/65dd7150b06f6c5cf345c70de8ca0348db62ef93/harnessbench/llm_judge.py" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">scoring implementation</a>, lines 327–371. The internal probe retains function text, inputs, actual outputs and hashes.</li><li>Maat maintainers, correction document captured in the same-day Research Object: <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Lorelys/maat-benchmarks/main/CORRECTIONS.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">halt audit and offline replay</a>. Author audits and replays are not treated as independent validation.</li><li>TARSphere research team, research paper, October 2026 version: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.30043" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">task state and metric definitions</a>, §§3–4. We ran no simulator and leave the installation-document/paper environment-version discrepancy unresolved.</li></ol><p>Cover: an editorial illustration of raw state and derived resolution, not an evaluation interface or experimental result figure.</p>',25))])}const v=r(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,v as default};
